5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:52,960
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and my guest today is Daphne Tideman, speaker, writer, and freelance growth advisor specializing in subscription apps and other D2C products.

7
00:00:53,360 --> 00:01:08,000
On the podcast, I talk with Daphne about why skipping user interviews is costing you growth, how to bring your product's aha moment forward into your marketing, and why your assumptions about why people use your app might be wrong.

8
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:11,680
Hey Daphne, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

9
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:12,800
Thanks for having me.

10
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,560
Really looking forward to chatting.

11
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:20,480
This topic is one that we've kind of touched on on the podcast before, but we haven't done a deep dive.

12
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:21,440
I think that's a shame.

13
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,120
So I'm excited to have you on and talk about jobs to be done.

14
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:28,800
You've done a webinar, some blog posts on the RevenueCat blog.

15
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,080
So let's say again, what is jobs to be done?

16
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:39,600
So it's definitely a great topic to talk about because it's something that once you understand it, it changes everything the way you see it.

17
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,360
And it's getting away from like, what is it that your app actually does?

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,160
And thinking instead like a job to be done is like, what is your end user trying to achieve?

19
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,600
What is their end goal?

20
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:56,480
And I always think of it as like they're going from A to B and you're helping them along the way in that journey.

21
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:12,640
And it's working out what that journey looks like and how can you support them to get there, rather than just thinking about, like, look, this is what we do, these are the features we have, because so many apps are constantly talking about, we have this feature, that feature, but that's not why people use your app.

22
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:14,720
How do you discover those jobs to be done?

23
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:19,440
How do you go through and figure out how people relate to your app in that way?

24
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:37,920
So I think the most important part of it in working it out is really talking to your end users and having those interviews and trying to understand, like, okay, what led you to actually using our app and just treating it again as like almost a journey that you came along halfway in that journey.

25
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,200
They probably didn't just suddenly start struggling in that very moment.

26
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:51,760
They've probably been trying to work it out for a while and trying to figure out, like, what was then the reason, the problem they were trying to solve and diving really deep into that as to what were they doing before that?

27
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,440
How were they solving it in the past?

28
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:57,840
And what then led them in that moment to think, hey, let me try out your app.

29
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,200
And that will really tell you, like, okay, what are they trying to solveve?

30
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,240
What are they trying to achieve?

31
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:07,520
And asking really about that outcome, what difference were they hoping to see by using your app?

32
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:10,640
That's for me the most valuable way of doing it.

33
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:15,360
But often, like, when I'm trying to work this out with a brand, like, I like to have multiple data points.

34
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:18,480
So the other thing I also like to do is go through all the reviews.

35
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:22,880
Obviously, there'll always be some reviews that are just purely about like the functionality of the app.

36
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:23,920
Hey, it crashed.

37
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:27,520
I'm unhappy about this or great app and that tells you nothing.

38
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:44,800
But I've also found that review mining and going through all the reviews and reading the more in-depth reviews of like, they say like, oh, I came across this because I was really struggling with my ADHD and I was trying to find a way to better focus and someone recommended this to me will also help you work out like, okay, what are they trying to achieve?

39
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:53,920
The other thing I also like to do is look at like, okay, with a survey data, working out who are the ones who are staying the longest.

40
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:58,240
So who's been subscribed for a while, who's actively using your app.

41
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:07,280
They're not just basically have a subscription they've forgotten about, but they're actively using your app, they're making the most of it and asking them through a survey.

42
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,360
That's really a good technique.

43
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:12,880
If you have a big database to make sure that your interviews are more focused..

44
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:20,000
But if I could choose one thing, I always say to interviews because that's everyone's least favorite thing because it costs most time, but it's the most valuable.

45
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:21,200
Yeah, totally.

46
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:43,920
When going through that discovery process, how do you think about kind of primary jobs to be done and secondary jobs to be done and how those relate to each other example I've given on the podcast before and what I think about constantly is, you know, I still have my side project weather app and it's interesting to me that, you know, there is a primary job to be done like, is it going to rain tomorrow?

47
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:45,680
You know, what's the weather like today?

48
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,760
And I can kind of go through and figure out some of those jobs to be done.

49
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:55,760
But then there's also like weird subtle jobs to be done where kind of weather is almost entertainment for folks.

50
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:08,160
Weather is a topic of discussion and part of the job to be done for some people using my weather app or any weather app is knowing the weather so that you can have a conversation about it later.

51
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:28,560
So, oh, do you know it's going to rain tomorrow or, or, gosh, wow, like it's going to be really cold next week or it's probably not something I would use for messaging, you know, like this is the best app to be able to talk about the weather, but it is kind of like part of the subtlety of how people think about using a weather app, even if it's not a primary.

52
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:41,920
So how do you think about those aspects of the primary jobs to be done and the secondary jobs to be done and even the, you know, apps where it's maybe not as task-oriented, but maybe the job to be done is a little more subtle.

53
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:50,960
So I think it's really good to get that layer deeper of like what is it that's actually driving them to come back because that's also for the retention side, hugely important.

54
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:56,000
And also in terms of your differentiator because a lot of apps can tell the weather.

55
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:03,120
So you just feel like messaging around like, hey, you know, you can find out what the weather is tomorrow and you're not going to stand out.

56
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,760
And I think it's actually in those secondary, more subtle things that you allow things to stand out.

57
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:13,600
So when we did the Pain Killer versus Vitamins webinar with Weltery, it's a health tracking app.

58
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:27,600
People love the data side of it, but it's actually making it more fun, making it almost like fun to check out and like little learning moments and almost as engaging as scrolling through like, you know, X and reading these little nuggets of wisdom that get people back.

59
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:30,720
So I think it's really good to know those secondary ones.

60
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:36,640
And even if you don't use it in your messaging, they might be what drives that retention because they might be that extra side of it.

61
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,040
it that creates an emotional connection.

62
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:52,640
And I think when we talk about jobs to be done, there's like the functional drivers, like purely like I'm trying to solve this problem, like I'm trying to stay fit, but there's also the emotional drivers, like I'm, you know, I'm not feeling as strong as I was.

63
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:57,280
I feel like I'm getting insecure about how I look and those emotional drivers.

64
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:01,040
I'm not saying like, misuse them in any way, but like, I think they're stronger.

65
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:03,840
They are so much stronger than the functional drivers.

66
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:08,320
So I think even if they are secondary, they might be stronger for you to get it back.

67
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:11,360
So I don't think you always have to pick one or the other.

68
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:12,560
I think it's good to understand.

69
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:18,960
both sides of it and to then work out like what do you want to like show also.

70
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:28,480
And that was also a really good thing that came out of the webinar when we were talking about it was this example of rather than trying to tell people with your messaging, like messaging is so much more than that.

71
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:36,080
It's not just the language, it's how you're positioning yourself, it's how you're bringing things over by showing them things rather than telling them.

72
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:47,200
And like going back to that Welltree example, they would basically pull in your data in the onboarding and you'd immediately get some insights about yourself in the onboarding rather than tell you we can give you insights.

73
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,480
It's nice for folks when they're thinking through this jobs to be done, discovery, like what are these jobs to be done?

74
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:58,320
You also mentioned social drivers.

75
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:02,320
So you have functional drivers, emotional drivers, and social drivers.

76
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:05,760
So what is that social driver piece and how should people be thinking about that?

77
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:10,480
Yes, so I think with the social side, it's like we can pretend that we're very rational people.

78
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,200
We really are.

79
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:17,040
Society's expectations and what others expect of us plays a role in our decision making.

80
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:19,440
And also that desire for connection.

81
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:24,560
And we see that also growing as a form of gamification in a lot of apps of like how do we connect?

82
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:34,160
with others that we might not even know that we might have never met and that that can also create a driver to do things like going back to like the workout.

83
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:38,320
It's the thing that we know we should do, but for a lot of us it is a struggle.

84
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:39,600
It is a struggle to do it.

85
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:44,160
And so having people hold you accountable and also appreciate it when you do things.

86
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:52,400
Like I don't very actively use Traverse, but I always get like kudos from like a few people who like always standardly are like, kind of supporting on there.

87
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:57,920
And I go on there to support them too, especially when I know they're training for a race or something or a competition.

88
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:03,680
Like, and that social aspect can also be a a driver and a reason why you use something.

89
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:16,320
So that's also a consideration, especially with apps where there's a community center or there's some fun little gamification about the social aspect that can be a big reason why they use your app is to feel that connection.

90
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:27,200
Yeah, the founder of Ladder Fitness app had him on the podcast two times now, but the most recent episode, he talked about how there's a new feature they added.

91
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:34,320
It's like a cheer feature where live while you're working out, other people can kind of cheer you on.

92
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:45,920
He said that they they ran the data on that and that people getting cheered on was highly correlated with additional retention or like some amount of increase in retention.

93
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:56,720
I forgot exactly the numbers he shared, but it's exactly what you're saying is that, you know, the latter app is now more retentive for incorporating those social dynamics into it.

94
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:59,360
And then, yeah, we see this a ton in community apps.

95
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:09,680
There's another health and fitness app I've had on the podcast, a food logging app, and they have a huge community and the community is a huge driver of that long term retention.

96
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:13,600
So, yeah, there's so many examples of how this social dynamic.

97
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:16,960
And again, it's you don't think of it as a job to be done.

98
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:23,760
You wouldn't necessarily say like the job to be done with ladder is to get cheered on in a workout.

99
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:24,000
No.

100
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:27,520
I mean, how would you reframe that then under this kind of social driver?

101
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:30,800
How would you frame that as a as a social job to be done?

102
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:32,320
Oh, that's a good one.

103
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:40,080
When I experience this, I want this so that I can it's usually the framework I use in framing these jobs to be done.

104
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,600
And so I would almost say like, let's take ladder as an example.

105
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:45,680
And I really can relate to this because I've had it.

106
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:48,800
I use Peloton and I've had it also on Peloton where like.

107
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:54,400
some random person claps me on and I just suddenly start biking fast then it's ridiculous because they can't see me.

108
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,360
They can make me see how many like output I've done but like I just like start biking faster.

109
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:12,480
So I believe this really works but I would say it's like, okay, when I want to let's say when I want to get stronger, I want to feel supported and motivated to work out so that I can stay consistent and see the results.

110
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:15,440
It's not the perfect framing but we're going on the spot here.

111
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:26,240
And I think like it's almost like the how how you get your users there is that social kind of driver is like if you understand what drives them, you can play on that help to help them achieve their job to be done.

112
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:34,800
If you understand that the social aspect of it matters to them or helps to keep them consistent and that accountability, then you can play on that.

113
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:47,520
So it might not even be in your job to be done sentence, but it might almost be like the understanding that that's what's driving them and knowing the underlying drivers of that job to be done might be what helps you then help them to achieve their goal.

114
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:48,640
If that makes sense.

115
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:58,720
So there's so many ways once you start to understand the job to be done for your app to then apply them across all different parts of the app.

116
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:07,040
I want to start with messaging and how to apply this understanding of jobs to be done to messaging.

117
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:24,000
And I really liked what you already said about, like, my weather app is that if all I'm messaging to people is you can check the weather, there's a million apps that can check the weather, and that's part of like understanding these jobs to be done is also helping identify those differentiating features.

118
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:31,120
So how do you work that into messaging and then how impactful can that be in more effective messaging?

119
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:31,840
Yeah.

120
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:39,040
So I think my first step is always narrowing it down because I think that's something that a lot of apps struggle with is that there's quite a few potential ones.

121
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:49,600
And then when they try to cover it all, it's really hard to get that in there because you, you know, you are more limited in like, okay, how many places and how much can I communicate?

122
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:59,040
And obviously, in the end of the day, you can always personalize it all or make different personalized flow, but you kind of want to have this starting point of like, what is my initial messaging?

123
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:02,880
So I always first with the jobs that we've done, try to narrow it down a bit.

124
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:08,720
You should have already narrowed it down by only talking to those like high spending users and those high potential ones.

125
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:12,560
But then I also look at like, what is the problem that we're trying to solve?

126
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:15,360
How good are we at actually solving that problem?

127
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:16,880
How good a solution are we for that?

128
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:19,840
And what chance is there that we can differentiate?

129
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:28,800
And that's where like another part of my research, which we haven't talked about yet, is comes in, which is competitor analysis and understanding which angles the competitors are going on.

130
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:37,440
Because back to the weather app, like if everyone's just saying, you know, check it within a moment or talk about the weather, like then you're not going to stand out on that.

131
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:38,960
And then I'll narrow that down.

132
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:44,720
And then what I'll do before I integrate it into all the messaging of the app itself is I'll test it first.

133
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:48,960
And if you're running ads, meta ads are great for this because whatever the weather is.

134
00:13:48,960 --> 00:13:59,840
Because what I've done in the past is I've literally tested very similar visuals with different messaging overlay on them and different messaging in the text and then tested those against each other.

135
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:10,400
First on the job to be done level with a more general image that isn't skewing people too much in one direction or another and then narrowing it down of like, do we want to talk about pains?

136
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:15,120
Do we want to talk about the gains of what people are trying to get in terms of benefits?

137
00:14:15,360 --> 00:14:17,680
Do we want to talk very literally about the jobs to be done?

138
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,720
Do we want to talk about the situations when you're experiencing this?

139
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:22,240
This could help you.

140
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:24,160
There's different ways of bringing it across.

141
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:37,120
So I tend to narrow that first down within the ads and everyone's always like, metas so visual, it's so visual, but it's insane, like the differences I've seen in like the click through rates just with like these different jobs to be done.

142
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:48,560
And then I start to see like, okay, how can I then start to not only be able to like what we talked about before, like bring it across in a copy way, but how can I bring it across in a visual way?

143
00:14:49,200 --> 00:15:00,400
And so when we're talking about messaging and integrating it, we need to be looking at every single visual we do, every single message we do, like how can we bring this across to the user in a way that then resonates with them?

144
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:21,920
And if you've done all that review mining and gone through all those reviews and kept a nice log of the reviews, and yes, you can speed it all up with AI, but I really believe strongly in also just spending a few hours just completely immersing yourself in the language, you'll have this extra benefit on top of that with the messaging for the copy side of it where suddenly your language becomes more human.

145
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:24,240
It starts to resonate more.

146
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:32,160
And so like I would stop really first with like the acquisition side, not to like improve acquisition, but to use it as a way to test different messaging.

147
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:36,960
And once you've tested that in a few different ways, you can also use emails for this..

148
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:40,160
If you have a good size email base, you can test with push notifications.

149
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:41,520
You can test this in different ways.

150
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:51,040
Once I start to get confident of, like, hey, we're consistently seeing this win, that's when I start to test integrating it step by step and see, like, how can we bring this better across.

151
00:15:51,760 --> 00:16:02,080
And you're actually looking at in meta, the different conversion rates and even the different monetization of those users depending on the way you frame the job to be done.

152
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:02,640
Yeah.

153
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:10,400
So I'm literally looking at, like, okay, if they have enough data and the cost per trial, for example, is low enough, then we can do it based on that.

154
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:11,280
That's the ideal.

155
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:12,480
If it's not or if there.

156
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:19,440
Or if they're a very new app and they don't have big budgets, then I'll look on click through rates and see what seems to be resonating better versus the rest.

157
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:27,840
I will say when I do it on a click through rate basis because click through rate doesn't always perfectly correlate, I will try to run a few tests and see if I can test it somewhere else.

158
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:38,480
I've also, by the way, like used, for example, usability hub to run five seconds tests with two messaging and ask people what their preferences are and why and like what stood out.

159
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:46,480
That's also really, really good in refining it because sometimes you use a word that just triggers people the wrong way or people don't understand what you mean.

160
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:47,680
You're trying to be too smart.

161
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:57,440
And so that can also like if you feel like, hey, I really don't seem to be getting it right, or I feel like there's something quite wrong, but I don't know what, of getting a bit more qualitative feedback on it.

162
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:01,200
How do you think about different personas to in ladder?

163
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:07,440
You know, my wife is a persona which is so different than like me as a persona.

164
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:15,440
And, you know, there's so many different people and those people do have different perceptions of those jobs to be done.

165
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:17,600
So, yeah, how do you hone in on that?

166
00:17:18,080 --> 00:17:24,640
Because even in the AB testing, maybe overall one message resonated more, but the other message.

167
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:28,240
did resonate well with a different audience.

168
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:30,320
What do you think about the personages in that?

169
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:33,360
The reality is we rarely have just one job to do.

170
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:38,320
And we rarely have just one way of resonating with that job to do.

171
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:45,280
So I first try to get the most out of that job to do because I think like people are very quick to try and do everything at the same time.

172
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:47,760
And I will build a persona around that job to do.

173
00:17:47,920 --> 00:17:48,320
I don't.

174
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:51,280
So for me, personas are not about fluffy demographics.

175
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:53,600
They're really about like what is the person trying to achieve?

176
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:54,560
What are their struggles?

177
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:55,440
What are their questions?

178
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,880
And so I'll first really try to get the most out of that job to do.

179
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:01,200
But then I'll go back and see see, like, okay.

180
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:11,040
Also again, based on that survey data, we probably had a few jobs to be done that were spending quite a bit that were retaining that seemed to love the app and using it actively.

181
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:17,280
So I'll go back and like interview a different group and try to work out, okay, what's different with them versus the other one?

182
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:22,080
And how can we basically then adjust the way we're doing things to them?

183
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:28,320
And I think that's a great thing, like about, like, if you're using ads, meta will find people who will resonate with your ads.

184
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:33,680
So you can have different groups of ads, one that's speaking to one job to be done, one that's speaking to another.

185
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:43,760
Where I think it gets a bit tricky is like, obviously you can use like custom like landers for them, like in terms of like what they're seeing in the app store or if you're doing web to app, it's you know, you can also do it there.

186
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:58,800
But where I think it gets a bit more tricky is like once they're in the app, I think that's where the personalization with an onboarding flow is really important that you're adjusting like the content that they're seeing and the experience they're having in that onboarding to the different jobs that we've done.

187
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:02,080
So like Headspace, for example, they're very focused on sleep.

188
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:08,400
But if you say you're struggling with anxiety and stress, you're going to see very different content in the app than if you click on see.

189
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:17,360
And I think that's how like apps can also adjust this, like making sure they're asking the right questions and onboarding to work out what, like, variant of that job to be done.

190
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:24,080
Are they talking to and what do they then need to show them to actually be able to onboard them and resonate with them.

191
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:26,800
Like they're probably going to be showing a different form of social proof.

192
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:29,520
They're probably going to be talking about different content and ad.

193
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:31,600
They're probably going to be using different language.

194
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:33,760
And I think that's where that onboarding comes in.

195
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:42,800
Like it's not about like learning for your sake, but it's learning for their sake so that you can still feel like it resonates with you in terms of that job to be done.

196
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:44,000
