Sorry.

354
00:35:01,200 --> 00:35:01,280
Yeah.

355
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:03,040
I haven't even used the app.

356
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:04,160
That's the worst part.

357
00:35:04,800 --> 00:35:06,000
It's so unintentional.

358
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:08,800
I've only used it once because it was just last week.

359
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:17,440
But in talking through that, it made me realize that the reason I didn't use it was because I've had lower back issues since my early twenties.

360
00:35:17,920 --> 00:35:22,000
And what I liked about Fitbot was that I was in control of the exercises.

361
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:24,720
I built my own plans and I built them around.

362
00:35:25,920 --> 00:35:33,760
knowing which exercises trigger my back and being careful not to do things where I know, you know, my form or whatever.

363
00:35:34,080 --> 00:35:38,400
So the aha moment for me in ladder, and I think this is a recent addition.

364
00:35:38,560 --> 00:35:52,160
And so this is maybe why but the aha moment was that they actually make it now so easy and honestly even better than Fitbot to customize the workout to where you can hit the right muscle group with like different exercises.

365
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:53,840
And it's really easy.

366
00:35:54,080 --> 00:36:00,720
Like the user experience of switching out the exercises to accommodate your needs is really, really good.

367
00:36:01,600 --> 00:36:02,720
And that was the aha moment.

368
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:07,680
It was when I was able to go through the workout and in like seconds customize it.

369
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:14,240
So I don't, you know, it's like, I don't know if they could, if that's a strong enough job to be done for enough people.

370
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:31,600
I mean, maybe I'll send this episode to Greg, the CEO, and tell him they should maybe do some experiments with the job to be done of people who, you know, have specific needs and are worried that a workout app is going to force them to do exercises that hurt them or, you know, are problematic.

371
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:36,640
Whereas now the app is so good at being able to just go through and make those customizations.

372
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:42,000
And I think that kind of touches on another key part of like, we've like a job to be done.

373
00:36:42,160 --> 00:36:44,640
Logically, we think everyone just wants to achieve that.

374
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:46,960
And if we show them they can achieve that, that they'll do that.

375
00:36:47,440 --> 00:36:51,840
But like you said, like, there's you had the concern up front, will this hurt my back?

376
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:55,360
You know, will I be able to create something that I need?

377
00:36:55,920 --> 00:36:58,800
And when you saw that that was the case, you were like, okay, great.

378
00:36:59,200 --> 00:36:59,920
Let me try this out.

379
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:01,520
That's the other thing I always ask people.

380
00:37:01,680 --> 00:37:04,240
It's like, okay, what almost stopped you from signing up?

381
00:37:04,640 --> 00:37:06,000
What kind of concerns did you have?

382
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:07,600
And sometimes people don't know it.

383
00:37:07,840 --> 00:37:10,160
And then I ask them, have you recommended this to friends and family?

384
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:12,720
And they'll say, like, you know, yeah, I recommended to this person.

385
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:20,480
I asked them what questions they asked you because they'll forget what they might have been worried about because they want to confirm like, hey, no, I made the right choice.

386
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:21,440
I wasn't worried.

387
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:26,800
But they'll remember the things that other people asked them, what came up because then it's like uncomfortable.

388
00:37:26,880 --> 00:37:28,960
It's like, oh, I encouraged you to do this.

389
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:31,360
And you're basically like, will it actually do this?

390
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:33,200
Did I actually know if it did that or not?

391
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:35,440
Because sometimes you can't answer those questions.

392
00:37:35,600 --> 00:37:51,760
And so I think that's also a key part of it is like, again, like it gives you so much content when you understand these jobs to be done because you not only talk to like showing the transformation, but you can also then lower in the funnel like talk to like, hey, all these different concerns, like can do certain exercises, no stress.

393
00:37:52,320 --> 00:37:56,160
You can customize it completely to what you need to prevent yourself getting hurt.

394
00:37:56,320 --> 00:38:01,200
Like there's so many different ways you can then bring that in and create also content around that.

395
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:08,320
But that's why I preach those interviews so much, because I'm like, you only realize this stuff by speaking and having these conversations.

396
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:14,240
That's such a great one too, of asking those questions of what stopped you.

397
00:38:14,880 --> 00:38:24,640
You know, if people don't end up in the app, I guess this is the more like user research kind of things where you're doing user research with people who have never used the app.

398
00:38:26,240 --> 00:38:32,960
And then you just go through and you walk them through the process and then ask those kind of questions or how do you get to these blocking points?

399
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:39,920
And I guess you could do it in the app at different stages of the funnel where people drop off the funnel, figuring out why they dropped off the funnel.

400
00:38:40,080 --> 00:38:42,240
But yeah, how do you figure out those like blockers?

401
00:38:42,640 --> 00:38:47,440
I probably combine it with something else that we're talking about these huge apps right now.

402
00:38:47,680 --> 00:38:54,880
And the reality is that most apps don't have a huge database always to work with, especially if they're just starting out.

403
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:57,040
And I work a lot with startups.

404
00:38:57,200 --> 00:38:59,040
So I really know and feel that pain.

405
00:39:00,320 --> 00:39:28,480
Like, if you want to like talk to people who've never got that far and like do those kinds of like interviews with them, I would look at like what other solutions fulfill your jobs to be done and then recruit users, for example, using a platform and I have no affiliate with them, but like, for example, user interviews dot com or respondent IO, like use a platform like that to recruit users who have used similar solutions in solving the jobs to be done and then ask them a question about like, okay, which of these is a pain point for you?

406
00:39:28,640 --> 00:39:35,280
And just give them a load of like bogus options in there that they don't that you're not asking them like, hey, have you struggled with this pain point?

407
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:35,840
Yes or no?

408
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:40,880
Because everyone just would just say yes then, but like making it more difficult for them to figure out what's the right answer.

409
00:39:41,440 --> 00:39:48,480
And then you can basically do like those interviews with those people who have never even converted, may not even know you yet.

410
00:39:48,960 --> 00:40:08,960
And first what I tend to do is work out their job to be done, again confirm, figure out like what, what solutions are they using, what do they like and dislike, where have they looked for alternatives, and then do exactly what you said, like a user test with them and walk through it, and then get them to talk out loud through their experience of it to see like, okay, where are they getting their hesitations?

411
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:10,000
What questions are they having?

412
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:10,800
What's not clear?

413
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:18,080
That tends to be something I do a little bit later in the process because I want to first work out, like, are we on the right track with this?

414
00:40:18,240 --> 00:40:26,000
And then once we've kind of validated, this is the messaging, this is the angle, we've improved our onboarding around this, have we actually done it well enough?

415
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:27,840
Have we actually got it across?

416
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:30,320
Where are the places that we're still not working out?

417
00:40:30,800 --> 00:40:32,960
Because you can do it before that, for sure too.

418
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:42,080
But I think it's so much more powerful to do it once you've actually understood what you're trying to bring across and you've refined that initially to really take it then to the next level.

419
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:48,240
Because again, I'm always thinking like, people won't want to do five rounds of this because everyone's always, you know.

420
00:40:48,240 --> 00:40:52,560
You know, doesn't have enough time to do this, but it is very impactful.

421
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:55,440
Yeah, but these are the things that make it break.

422
00:40:55,840 --> 00:40:58,400
I mean, just talking through this is just a reminder to me.

423
00:40:58,560 --> 00:41:06,000
I've done some user testing in the past, but I haven't done it recently, and I'm not talking to users in my side project app.

424
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:06,400
Why?

425
00:41:06,480 --> 00:41:07,120
Because it's hard.

426
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:08,160
I have a full time job.

427
00:41:08,640 --> 00:41:13,760
It's like I squeeze in a few hours a week, if that, but these are the things that make it break.

428
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:18,080
This is how you grow and become a much better, stronger app.

429
00:41:18,800 --> 00:41:25,360
It's not just your own individual product intuition, but it's actually a fantastic reminder.

430
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:28,320
And for those people listening and thinking like, I don't have time.

431
00:41:28,480 --> 00:41:28,960
This is too hard.

432
00:41:29,200 --> 00:41:44,640
It's like, well, if you're struggling and you're spending hours and hours and hours and hundreds of hours on product development, you're spending hundreds of hours on marketing, you're spending hundreds of hours on other things, like this is going to supercharge all of those things going out and talking to users.

433
00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:47,520
And yes, it's hard, but you have to figure it out.

434
00:41:48,080 --> 00:41:51,440
It will also really save you time because you'll end up doing less features.

435
00:41:51,600 --> 00:41:54,320
You'll remove things that aren't helping people on that journey.

436
00:41:54,640 --> 00:42:00,240
Like you end up refining your product roadmap so much because of it versus trying to test everything.

437
00:42:00,960 --> 00:42:03,360
You'll get really much smarter in terms of testing.

438
00:42:03,760 --> 00:42:06,800
And the challenge I'm going to give you for this is because it's a weather app, right?

439
00:42:07,040 --> 00:42:07,680
It's helping people.

440
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:18,480
So you can literally go out to a park and just put it in people's hands and offer to buy them a coffee from a local cafe or go into a cafe and do it there.

441
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:22,000
I had an app that's like a 24/7 app.

442
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:24,800
I love working with dog brands because I have a dog.

443
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:38,000
So not a doggy sample, but it basically you can just send in like a short video, short audio note if you're having a problem with your dog or cat or whatever pet and they'll get back to you within minutes and let you know like hey to you, do you really need to go to a vet?

444
00:42:38,240 --> 00:42:38,880
What should you do?

445
00:42:39,120 --> 00:42:48,480
So if you're, if your dog eats something in the park, you don't have to rush immediately most of the time, but if you don't know that, you kind of like do it out of panic and spend hundreds.

446
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:59,760
And so for this app, like, I basically went into my dog park and just asked a lot of people there who were walking their dogs, we walked the dog together, strangers I'd never met before, and just asked them to test the app.

447
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:05,200
And I said I'd give them a year free of it as a thank you, like a year subscription for free as a thank you for it.

448
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:10,480
And I just asked them to go through it, sign up and just send them one random question they have on their mind about their pet.

449
00:43:10,720 --> 00:43:12,000
And if they didn't know, I'd give them one.

450
00:43:12,560 --> 00:43:18,480
And I just go through that flow and I found so many issues and so many things where it just wasn't resonating, bringing things across.

451
00:43:18,960 --> 00:43:20,480
It was clumsy, it wasn't working.

452
00:43:20,880 --> 00:43:26,160
And it took me just like an hour or two of walking around the park with my dog to get like so many insights.

453
00:43:26,320 --> 00:43:27,680
I just put my phone recorder on.

454
00:43:27,840 --> 00:43:31,280
Like it was very like low key, nothing fancy, you know.

455
00:43:31,440 --> 00:43:32,880
So that's the other thing I would say.

456
00:43:33,040 --> 00:43:37,280
Like if you say you don't have time for this, next time you're in a cafe, just start a conversation.

457
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:45,280
It's scary for the first two times, but I promise you, people actually like half the time find it really intriguing and just like, kind of go along with it.

458
00:43:45,600 --> 00:43:48,640
I've done it even for like non apps, like a pension grant too.

459
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:56,560
Or just talk to random people, a cafe, buy a lot of people coffees and learn so much from all these random people about like how the site was performing.

460
00:43:56,880 --> 00:44:08,880
I'm sure a lot of people will be mortified by that idea, but maybe you can hire someone who is more outgoing and have them video record the conversation or something, but I did want to move on to activation.

461
00:44:09,360 --> 00:44:26,640
So we spent a ton of time talking about getting the messaging right for ads, for screenshots, for that initial messaging, but then how do you translate that into the activation moment and how do you iterate on that to really dial in the activation using jobs to be done?

462
00:44:27,280 --> 00:44:27,360
Yeah.

463
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:37,280
So I think when it comes to the activation side, it's hopefully if you've already integrated this before, you've already kind of built that trust of like, hey, you're going to have this at a moment.

464
00:44:37,760 --> 00:44:43,200
But it's like, okay, how do you now bring this over and how do you start to like build a habit around this?

465
00:44:43,760 --> 00:44:57,360
And I think what's really key with this is understanding like what could prevent them from actually consistently using it and how can you make it easier or funner to actually do it because I think for a lot of jobs to be done, like we don't, we say we want to do one thing.

466
00:44:57,840 --> 00:45:00,880
And this is something, again, we talked about in that painkiller vitamin webinar.

467
00:45:01,200 --> 00:45:07,360
bit is like we say we want to, you know, practice gratitude, but do we actually take the time to do it?

468
00:45:08,320 --> 00:45:11,840
We say we want to learn a language, but do we actually take the time to do it?

469
00:45:12,320 --> 00:45:22,720
And so working out what those kind of like barriers of them achieving it in the past, like what's the reasons they failed to achieve that job to be done in the past, can then help you work out like what is it that I need to integrate into that?

470
00:45:23,120 --> 00:45:32,000
And that's where I think gamification often comes in and makes it even more fun or making it more engaging to basically achieve that and have that sense of progress.

471
00:45:32,720 --> 00:45:36,560
And so I think your whole activation is about giving them that feeling of progress and getting there.

472
00:45:37,360 --> 00:45:46,000
So like I was doing some work with like a gratitude app and like one of the ideas we talked about is like, well, why don't we do like a three day challenge potentially in the onboarding already?

473
00:45:46,560 --> 00:45:54,640
And then actually have them already do that first gratitude practice so that they feel like I've already achieved something, I've made progress already.

474
00:45:54,960 --> 00:46:01,680
And I think like that's why like a lot of these like language apps like will immediately try to get you to do your first lesson and just make it as small as possible.

475
00:46:02,240 --> 00:46:12,560
So I think bringing it into their activation is like what is the tinyest step you can get them to do to make them feel like they're making progress from A to B, even if it's a false sense of progression.

476
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:24,640
With a workout, you might not immediately be able to work out in that moment, but is the feeling that you have a plan in place, that you have clarity, does that almost give you that takeoff feeling?

477
00:46:24,880 --> 00:46:32,080
And I'm not talking about like just streaks or that because I think they can be quite flawed as a mechanism, but that's a different topic.

478
00:46:32,560 --> 00:46:48,560
But it's that feeling that you progress, that you've done this tiny thing, whether even if it's just planning it, like what gives people the feeling that they're moving towards achieving that job to be done, even if it's a false sense of progress, but it's just giving them that feeling of, let's help you get there.

479
00:46:49,200 --> 00:47:02,320
You keep saying false sense of progress, but, but honestly, I think this is one of those subtle secondary jobs to be done is that, you know, what's one of the jobs to be done of a gym membership?

480
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:15,760
Part of the job to be done is making you feel guilty that you're not using it and paying fifty, sixty dollars a month and never going is part of the job to be done because that's highly motivating.

481
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:16,720
I'm spending money.

482
00:47:17,280 --> 00:47:19,600
I'm wasting money by not going to the gym.

483
00:47:20,160 --> 00:47:31,680
So even like you were saying with a fitness app or even the gratitude app, I think that's really cool how in the onboarding you could even ask the questions and this would be, you know, real direct progress.

484
00:47:32,160 --> 00:47:33,280
What are you grateful for today?

485
00:47:34,160 --> 00:47:39,680
It's almost like you can page by page step them through what is the gratitude practice.

486
00:47:39,840 --> 00:47:44,720
And then at the end, like, congrats, you've done it, you've successfully completed day one.

487
00:47:45,280 --> 00:47:46,720
Now move on to day two.

488
00:47:46,880 --> 00:47:51,200
And, and, but you can work it in in more subtle ways to help them make progress.

489
00:47:51,920 --> 00:48:03,760
And then, you know, like you said with the health and fitness app, the plan is, is progress, like having a plan in place, having being ready to work out versus just showing up and like having to figure it out on the spot.

490
00:48:03,920 --> 00:48:14,800
I mean, again, that brings this back to me and Ladder, but that was part of my problem with Fitbot was that they do have an automatic function that automatically generates a workout, but I never liked the workout.

491
00:48:15,040 --> 00:48:21,680
So then every time I would go workout, I would go through and have to customize and customizing the workout would take a long time.

492
00:48:22,480 --> 00:48:29,920
So then I created some preset workouts, but then I only had two preset workouts and I alternate between them and then I got bored of those two workouts.

493
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:45,120
And so having that plan in place and that's what's now the killer job to be done about Ladder is that I have a personal trainer super experienced, super talented, preselecting all the workouts for the week.

494
00:48:45,440 --> 00:48:51,200
All I have to do is show up and then it only takes me a couple of seconds to customize it and then I'm on and going.

495
00:48:51,520 --> 00:48:58,480
And so having that plan in place is part of the job to be done of a fitness app, of a meditation app, of many of these apps.

496
00:48:58,880 --> 00:49:04,400
There probably are examples of false progress as well, but I think progress towards the goal is progress.

497
00:49:05,120 --> 00:49:09,680
So yeah, help people along the way and then celebrate that they've made some progress.

498
00:49:10,240 --> 00:49:12,000
Yeah, I think I'm just trying to think of the fact.

499
00:49:12,160 --> 00:49:16,000
The fact so I work a lot with wellness and like eco friendly kind of brands.

500
00:49:16,240 --> 00:49:30,800
So it's a bit easier in that space to have immediate progress, but I am also very conscious that there are also different types of apps where the end goal or where you want to get to might be a bit bigger or you might not be able to do it at the moment of downloading.

501
00:49:31,120 --> 00:49:34,160
So let's say you've got an app that's helping you with doing your tax return.

502
00:49:34,880 --> 00:49:39,680
You might download it with like, hey, I really need to do this, but I don't need to do it right now.

503
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:43,840
But in your mind, doing the job is doing the tax return.

504
00:49:44,000 --> 00:49:47,360
Like, and that's what you need to work out is like, what does progress look like for them?

505
00:49:48,000 --> 00:49:55,120
And you can then reshape it because like you're saying like, hey, I appreciate that having the plan is part of it, but not everyone might see or understand that.

506
00:49:55,440 --> 00:50:02,160
So I think it's like trying to work out with like that initial moment, because I think my activation is longer than that just initial moment.

507
00:50:02,400 --> 00:50:07,200
But I think it's so important with that initial step of like showing them like, hey, you are one step closer.

508
00:50:07,680 --> 00:50:09,920
Like I think LinkedIn also does this really well.

509
00:50:10,080 --> 00:50:12,960
It's like where they show you like your profile is X percent complete.

510
00:50:13,040 --> 00:50:19,440
So it's not a huge task of doing the whole profile, but it's I've already filled in a bit, you know, I'm leveling up.

511
00:50:19,600 --> 00:50:21,920
I'm improving it step by step with my tax return.

512
00:50:22,240 --> 00:50:34,080
I might not be able to do it yet, but maybe I've got a clear idea and I've completed step one because I know which documents I need or I've set a reminder like a push notification that I'm going to do in this moment, like put it in my calendar.

513
00:50:34,480 --> 00:50:43,760
I think it's just trying to figure out if you can't get them to do it in that moment because a lot of people download apps on the go, you know, they see it, they think it's interesting, they're like, Oh, I'll try it out sometime.

514
00:50:44,400 --> 00:50:55,120
How can you still get them in those few minutes that you might have while they're commuting or doing whatever, get them to feel like this is actually going to help me achieve what I want to achieve.

515
00:50:55,760 --> 00:51:06,800
And then the retention side of it is really interesting because that's what you also we also forget, it's like our jobs to be done and our knowledge, they evolve over time.

516
00:51:07,600 --> 00:51:14,480
So I've been using a recipe app for a long time to work up recipes, but they just became repetitive.

517
00:51:14,640 --> 00:51:15,360
They became similar.

518
00:51:15,920 --> 00:51:17,840
They didn't feel as inspiring anymore.

519
00:51:18,000 --> 00:51:30,240
And that actually led me to try out a few other different recipe apps because I had gotten to a point where I didn't feel inspired the way I did before or maybe my cooking is also like evolved in like, hey, I want to try more new things or do different things.

520
00:51:30,480 --> 00:51:31,680
Like I love cooking anyway.

521
00:51:31,920 --> 00:51:35,600
So I wanted something that was like challenging me in a way that it doesn't anymore.

522
00:51:35,840 --> 00:51:38,400
And I think we're going to use a different fitness app for once.

523
00:51:38,640 --> 00:51:39,200
Sorry, ladder.

524
00:51:39,680 --> 00:51:40,800
Really happy to still try it out.

525
00:51:41,120 --> 00:51:49,360
But sweat is a really good example of this because I used them just when I was just starting to get into working out.

526
00:51:49,920 --> 00:51:56,800
Like I'd been running a bit before that, but like I'd not been very consistent in it and I really wanted to see a difference in it.

527
00:51:57,200 --> 00:52:04,160
And they had all these programs that each time I could go from like beginner, intermediate, advanced and really feel like I was getting to a next level.

528
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:08,640
And I think like that's we go from like activation flowing into the retention side.

529
00:52:08,720 --> 00:52:11,920
